# ChimeraX scene: IL-6 (1ALU) with designed binder short_9
#
# Run from the directory holding il6_binder_site3_short_9.pdb:
#     open view_short9.cxc
# or in ChimeraX:  File > Open > this file
#
# NOTE: chain B is RFdiffusion output, which is BACKBONE ONLY. Every residue
# is a glycine placeholder, so there are no side chains to show on the binder.
# Judge its shape from the cartoon, not from its chemistry.

open il6_binder_site3_short_9.pdb

# --- base scene -------------------------------------------------------------
hide atoms
show cartoon
set bgColor white
lighting soft
graphics silhouettes true

color /A gray(70)
color /B cornflowerblue

# --- IL-6 surface, so you can see where the binder actually lands -----------
surface /A
transparency /A 60 surfaces

# --- the hotspots we conditioned on -----------------------------------------
# A47 (helix A tail), A155/A157/A158 (helix D start)
show /A:47,155,157,158 atoms
style /A:47,155,157,158 stick
color /A:47,155,157,158 orange

# Trp157: the dominant site III hotspot, ~25% of buried surface there
color /A:157 red
label /A:157 residues text "W157"

# --- the binder residues we measured within 10 A of IL-6 --------------------
# 3-15 contiguous (helix 1 lying along the target), 18, then 27/31/34/38
# (one face of helix 2). 19-26 is the turn and touches nothing.
color /B:3-15,18 gold
color /B:27,31,34,38 yellow

view

# ============================================================================
# Extras. Paste these one at a time in the ChimeraX command line.
# ============================================================================
#
# Is it really a helical hairpin? Strip the target and look at the binder alone.
# My claim from the sequence periodicity was two amphipathic helices packing
# together. This is the check:
#     ~surface /A
#     hide /A cartoon
#     view /B
#
# Put the target back:
#     show /A cartoon
#     surface /A
#     transparency /A 60 surfaces
#     view
#
# Colour the binder by secondary structure, to count the helices:
#     color /B byattribute ss_type
#
# How buried is W157 by the binder?
#     surface /A enclose /A
#     measure buriedarea /A /B
#
# Save a figure:
#     save short9_siteIII.png width 1600 supersample 3
